{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"old electronics\" recycle donate sell wipe)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#5_1150498289","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3596","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#5_1150498289\" , \"end_char\": 4003.000000 , \"headings\": \"How to Get Rid of Your Old Electronics How to Get Rid of Your <cover>Old Electronics Advertiser Disclosure ▾ Wise Bread Picks Safety First Sell It Trade It In Donate It Recycle It Like this article? Pin it! \" , \"segment\": \"When it comes to your cell phone, you also need to ensure that your account with your service provider has been terminated. If you aren't sure how to wipe </cover>an electronic device (hint: it involves more than just deleting your files), you may want to consult a professional (or tech-savvy friend) for advice. Leaving personal data on a device could make your private life more public than you'd like — and put you at risk for identity theft. Sell It If your old devices still work, you could try selling them on eBay, Craigslist, or through a local classified ad. There are also several services that are willing to pay cash for certain gadgets. Nextworth, Gazelle and ReCellular all offer such programs. "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"5/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#5_1150498289","relevance":3,"reason":"The page is dedicated to 'How to Get Rid of Your Old Electronics' and gives direct disposal steps including safety/data wiping, selling, trading in, donating, and recycling devices.","learned":"Individuals should terminate service accounts where applicable, wipe devices beyond merely deleting files, and then sell, trade in, donate, or recycle old electronics."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"This query produced a highly relevant consumer guidance page with multiple concrete disposal options, so continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#4_1150496708","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":2,"snippet":"v2.1_doc_58_809301304#4_1150496708\" , \"end_char\": 3512.000000 , \"headings\": \"How to Get Rid of Your Old Electronics How to Get Rid of Your <cover>Old Electronics Advertiser Disclosure ▾ Wise Bread Picks Safety First Sell It Trade It In Donate It Recycle It Like this article? Pin it! \" , \"segment\": \"And while that huge, old computer monitor may seem innocent enough, it's packed with lead and other toxic chemicals, which isn't just bad for the environment, it's bad for us too. So what can you do with your electronics when they're no longer of use? Here are a few environmentally friendly options. ( See also: 25 Things to Throw Out Today) Safety First First things first — whether you're offloading an old cell phone to a recycling service or selling a laptop to a friend, you need to wipe </cover>any and all electronic devices that store data. When it comes to your cell phone, you also need to ensure that your account with your service provider has been terminated. If you aren't sure how to wipe an "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"6/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#4_1150496708","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the same dedicated Wise Bread page on getting rid of old electronics, directly advising users to wipe all data-storing devices, terminate phone service accounts, and use environmentally friendly options such as selling, trading in, donating, or recycling. It overlaps with the prior Wise Bread result but still directly answers the need.","learned":"Before selling, recycling, or otherwise offloading electronics, individuals should wipe devices that store data and terminate related service accounts such as cell phone plans."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"Although the last result was a duplicate segment, the query is on target and may yield more dedicated old-electronics disposal pages; continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_274921401#1_631776794","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_274921401#1_631776794\" , \"end_char\": 1217.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Should You Do With Old Cell Phones? What Should You Do With Old Cell Phones? What to Do With Old Phones 1. Throw Your Old Phone Away (Not Recommended) 2. <cover>Recycle Your Phone 3. Repurpose Your Phone 4. Donate Your Phone 5. Sell Your Old Phone \" , \"segment\": \"If you’ve been wondering what you can do with your old cell phones, read on. What to Do With Old Phones The answer regarding what to do with old phones is similar to knowing what to do with old electronics in general. The first thing you’ll want to do is wipe </cover>any personal data from the phone. Whatever happens next, you don’t want that data out there where anyone who gets their hands on the phone can access it. There are several simple ways to wipe your data. The exception here is if you decide to keep this phone as a backup to your existing phone. You may want your data preserved "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"7/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_274921401#1_631776794","relevance":3,"reason":"The page is dedicated to what individuals should do with old cell phones, a common electronic device, and gives direct steps: wipe personal data first, then recycle, repurpose, donate, or sell rather than throw away.","learned":"For old phones, individuals should first wipe personal data, then consider recycling, repurposing, donating, or selling the device; throwing it away is not recommended."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The query is still returning dedicated disposal guidance for consumer electronics, so continue to collect more grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_2986316#4_6425314","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":4,"snippet":"your Mac How to <cover>recycle your Mac Don’t Throw It Away Erase Your Hard Drive Donate It Sell It Craft It Send It Back to Apple Find a Third-Party Recycler Plan Ahead \" , \"segment\": \"Next, wipe your hard drive. Track down the installation discs that came with your computer (or use the ones that came with any OS X upgrade you purchased). Insert Disc 1 (CD) or the installer disc (DVD), launch Installer, and reboot the machine using the software on the disc. Go to the Installer menu, choose Open Disk Utility, select the hard-drive icon in the column on the left, click on the Erase tab, and then click on Options. The 8 Way Random Write Format will scramble your data nicely. Now that your soon-to-be-departed Mac is wiped clean, it’s ready for recycling or donation. Donate It If your computer is still in working order, consider donating it. The research firm Dataquest estimates that less than 5 percent of all personal computers find a second life at a charity or school. You can increase that percentage by recycling your old electronics </cover>with a nonprofit facility. Such "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"8/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_2986316#4_6425314","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to recycling a Mac, an electronic device, and provides concrete disposal steps: do not throw it away, erase the hard drive, then donate, sell, send it back to Apple, or use a third-party recycler.","learned":"For computers such as Macs, individuals should securely erase the hard drive before recycling or donation, and can donate, sell, return to the manufacturer, or use a third-party recycler."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The current query continues to return dedicated guidance on disposing of old electronics, so continue to gather additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#3_1150494965","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":5,"snippet":"<cover>Old Electronics Advertiser Disclosure ▾ Wise Bread Picks Safety First Sell It Trade It In Donate It Recycle It Like this article? Pin it! \" , \"segment\": \"As it turns out, that's increasingly rare. According to the Consumer Electronics Association, American consumers consistently spend more than $1,000 per year on household electronics like televisions, computers, and smartphones. That's a lot of money, but assuming we all have the money to pay for these gadgets, what's more distressing is what happens to the older, less-advanced devices they aim to replace. To be blunt, most of these discarded gadgets end up in landfills, says the Environmental Protection Agency. In fact, it is estimated that Americans throw out more than 350,000 cell phones and 130,000 computers every day, making electronic waste, or \\\"e-waste,\\\" one of the fastest growing components of landfill waste. And while that huge, old computer monitor may seem innocent enough, it's packed with lead and other toxic chemicals, which isn't just bad for the environment, it's bad for us too. So what can you do with your electronics when they're no longer of use? </cover>"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"9/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_809301304#3_1150494965","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the same dedicated Wise Bread page 'How to Get Rid of Your Old Electronics.' Even though the snippet is mostly introductory and overlaps with previously judged segments, it frames the exact question and includes the page's disposal steps in the headings: safety first, sell, trade in, donate, and recycle.","learned":"Old electronics should not be landfilled because of e-waste and toxic materials; recommended options include wiping/safety steps followed by selling, trade-in, donation, or recycling."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The query remains productive for dedicated old-electronics disposal guidance, though some duplicates are appearing; continue to see if more distinct grade-3 results follow."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1377579587#9_2864450691","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":6,"snippet":"cash. If you want to sell it yourself, head to Craigslist, Amazon or eBay. For hassle-free trade-ins, use Gazelle. Donate your old devices to a cause you care about. If it’s broken or just too old to <cover>sell or donate, dispose of it safely. Don’t forget to cancel unnecessary subscriptions. Bonus Tip: Back up your data, then delete everything. Don’t those old electronics get dusty in your junk drawer – recycle them! Why not make some money while doing your part to make the world a better place? \" , \"segment\": \"Bonus Tip: Back up your data, then delete everything. If you’re planning on re-selling or donating your old devices, it’s important you take some time to retrieve any important stuff, like photos, passwords, contacts and music, that you want to keep. Once you’ve backed up all this info or transferred it to your new devices, wipe </cover>the old ones completely clean. You don’t want the new owner of your old phone to be able to see all the weird selfies you take in the bathroom at work, and you REALLY don’t want someone to have "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"10/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1377579587#9_2864450691","relevance":3,"reason":"The result directly advises what to do with old electronic devices: sell them, use trade-ins, donate them, safely dispose/recycle broken or too-old devices, cancel subscriptions, back up data, and wipe devices clean before resale or donation.","learned":"Individuals should back up important data, wipe devices completely, cancel unnecessary subscriptions, then sell, trade in, donate, or recycle/dispose safely depending on device condition."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The query is still producing direct old-electronics disposal guidance, so continue to collect additional grade-3 evidence, while watching for duplicate Wise Bread segments."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_405724282#10_847919122","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":7,"snippet":"<cover>sell or donate, dispose of it safely. Don't forget to cancel unnecessary subscriptions. Bonus Tip: Back up your data, then delete everything. \" , \"segment\": \"1.800.Recycling.com -- A recycling information site that partners with Best Buy, this service will help you find a place to recycle any kind of electronic device, no matter how strange. Earth911 -- Just type in what you need to get rid of and your zip code and you'll get a bunch of recycling options right in your neighborhood. Greener Gadgets -- Run by the Consumer Electronics Association, this site partners with electronics companies like Panasonic, Samsung, Staples and Amazon to ensure old devices don't go in the trash. Don't forget to cancel unnecessary subscriptions. Maybe you had a mobile data service connected with your old iPad, or maybe you're switching from an Xbox to a PS4 and don't need that Xbox Live Gold membership anymore. You don't want to be paying monthly fees for something you're not using, so make sure you cancel any subscriptions or services you don't need on your new devices. Bonus Tip: Back up your data, then delete </cover>"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"11/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_405724282#10_847919122","relevance":3,"reason":"The result gives direct steps for disposing of old electronic devices, including using recycling-location services such as 1-800-Recycling, Earth911, and Greener Gadgets, canceling unneeded subscriptions/services, backing up data, and deleting it before disposal.","learned":"Individuals can find electronics recyclers through services like Earth911 or 1-800-Recycling, should avoid putting devices in the trash, cancel device-related services, and back up then delete data."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The current query is still producing directly relevant old-electronics disposal guidance with concrete steps; continue to look for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_274921401#0_631775342","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":8,"snippet":"Cell Phones? What to Do With Old Phones 1. Throw Your Old Phone Away (Not Recommended) 2. <cover>Recycle Your Phone 3. Repurpose Your Phone 4. Donate Your Phone 5. Sell Your Old Phone \" , \"segment\": \"What Should You Do With Old Cell Phones? - The Whiz Cells Sell Your Smartphones Tech What Should You Do With Old Cell Phones? By The Whiz Cells March 17, 2017 No Comments “What should I do with my old cell phone?” It’s a question that’s asked often since when you’re ready for a new cell phone, you’ll likely want to get rid of the old one. It takes up space, and it can even get confusing if you keep picking up your old phone by accident. If you’ve been wondering what you can do with your old cell phones, read on. What to Do With Old Phones The answer regarding what to do with old phones is similar to knowing what to do with old electronics in general. The first thing you’ll want to do is wipe </cover>any personal data from the phone. Whatever happens next, you don’t want that data out there "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_274921401#0_631775342","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of a dedicated page on what to do with old cell phones, directly stating that the same principles apply to old electronics generally: wipe personal data first, then recycle, repurpose, donate, or sell the phone rather than throwing it away.","learned":"For old phones and similar electronics, individuals should wipe personal data before choosing reuse/disposal options such as recycling, repurposing, donating, or selling."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The current query continues to produce grade-3 disposal guidance, though with some duplicate segments; continue to see whether more distinct relevant documents remain."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1407188190#2_2920311122","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":9,"snippet":"<cover>Donate It Gift It \" , \"segment\": \"Sell It Believe it or not, there’s a robust market for old tech. Between Craigslist, Decluttr, eBay, Facebook Marketplace, Letgo, OfferUp, and Swappa, you should have no trouble finding someone to take an unwanted computer off your hands. On eBay, a nonfunctioning MacBook Pro recently sold for $206 and a third-generation iPad Mini for $188. More on Laptops How to Recycle Old Electronics CR's Laptop Ratings & Buying Guide Best Laptops of 2021 Laptops for $600 or Less Best Chromebooks of 2021 Online marketplaces make it really easy to list items, offering intuitive step-by-step instructions to help you sell your wares without a fee. Though eBay takes a small cut of the sale price, it's likely to yield a bigger sum than buy-back sites such as BuyBackWorld, Gazelle, Glyde, and ItsWorthMore. Before you sell a digital device to someone you don't know, though, take a few precautions to protect your privacy. You don't want bank account info, personal images, or your browsing history lingering on the machine. With a tablet, a factory reset (activated through the Settings menu) will wipe </cover>the "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1407188190#2_2920311122","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is dedicated to options for old tech/electronics and gives direct disposal steps such as selling, donating or gifting, with privacy precautions like factory resetting tablets before transferring them to someone else.","learned":"Individuals can sell old electronics through marketplaces or buy-back sites, but should protect privacy first by wiping devices, such as using a factory reset on tablets."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596","reason":"The query remains productive for direct old-electronics disposal guidance, so continue retrieving further results while avoiding overemphasis on duplicate segments."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3596"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1377579587#8_2864448346","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3596","rank":10,"snippet":"<cover>sell or donate, dispose of it safely. Don’t forget to cancel unnecessary subscriptions. Bonus Tip: Back up your data, then delete everything. Don’t those old electronics get dusty in your junk drawer – recycle them! Why not make some money while doing your part to make the world a better place? \" , \"segment\": \"Earth911 — Just type in what you need to get rid of and your zip code and you’ll get a bunch of options for recycling old electronics right in your neighborhood. Greener Gadgets — Run by the Consumer Technology Association, this site partners with electronics companies like Panasonic, Samsung, Staples and Amazon to ensure old devices don’t go in the trash. Don’t forget to cancel unnecessary subscriptions. Maybe you had a mobile data service connected with your old iPad, or maybe you’re switching from an Xbox to a PS4 and don’t need that Xbox Live Gold membership anymore. You don’t want to be paying monthly fees for something you’re not using, so make sure you cancel any subscriptions or services you don’t need on your new devices. Bonus Tip: Back up your data, then delete </cover>"}}
