{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1675264766#6_3315062591","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the dedicated Earth911 page on removing data before electronics recycling. It gives concrete steps such as overwriting/encrypting data, factory-resetting cellphones, removing and cutting SIM cards, and seeking help if users cannot erase devices themselves before recycling or donating.","learned":"For phones and other electronics, individuals should delete data, follow manufacturer factory-reset instructions, remove and destroy SIM cards when applicable, and get help with erasure if needed before recycling or donation."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3597","reason":"The query is still producing highly relevant electronics disposal/data-security guidance, including authoritative references, so continue to retrieve additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1932927485#4_3118383344","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3597","rank":7,"snippet":"3297.000000 , \"headings\": \"Goodwill Electronics: The Place Where You Can Consciously Recycle E-Waste Goodwill Electronics: The Place Where You Can Consciously <cover>Recycle E-Waste About Goodwill Goodwill Electronics & Recycling Facts about E-waste What Can You Donate? Residential & Office/Business Personal Data Notice Goodwill Electronics & Dell Reconnect Is Donating My Old Computer Worth It? \" , \"segment\": \"E-waste currently represents more than 3% of America’s trash in landfills. At the same time, it accounts for 70% of overall toxic waste. People dispose of 20 to 50 million metric tons of e-waste worldwide every year. Cell phones and other electronics contain valuable amounts of gold or silver. Americans throw away phones containing over $60 million in gold/silver annually. We can recover 35,274 lbs of copper, 772 lbs of silver, 75 lbs of gold, and 33 lbs of palladium for every 1 million cell phones that are recycled. According to the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), </cover>e-waste is still the fastest growing municipal waste stream in America. Manufacturing a single computer and monitor takes 530 lbs of fossil fuel, 48 lbs of chemicals, and 1."}}
