{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_883613142#3_1781756239","relevance":3,"reason":"This EPA certified electronics recyclers page directly advises households with used electronics to donate or recycle through collection programs that use certified recyclers, to follow electronics donation/recycling tips before doing so, and lists options such as GreenerGadgets, Call2Recycle, municipal/local facilities, and SMM Electronics Challenge participants.","learned":"Households should use certified collection/recycling programs for electronics, consult donation/recycling tips before turning devices in, and can use options such as GreenerGadgets, Call2Recycle, municipal facilities, and EPA-linked programs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3597","reason":"The query continues to return authoritative, directly relevant electronics recycling and disposal guidance; continue to seek additional grade-3 evidence until results become irrelevant or exhausted."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3597"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_828461508#2_1571848270","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3597","rank":13,"snippet":"in Third World Countries Poor E-Waste Management Effects Data Security Through Improper <cover>Data Destruction SEE ALL GREEN POPULAR RECYCLING SERVICES: Let Us Contact You READ THESE SUCCESS STORIES \" , \"segment\": \"Improper Electronic Waste Disposal Leads to Negative Effects of E-waste on Environment How can e waste affect the environment? Computers contain toxic materials like zinc, nickel, lead, barium and chromium, which is why it’s critical to do proper computer recycling. Each of these metals occur in concentrations that are higher than the federally regulated levels. Our growing stream of e-waste is a threat to our environment. In 2005 an estimated 5.3 billion pounds of electronic waste was generated (EPA, 2005). Of this, only 9% was recycled (Yadong et al., 2006). When we retire our computers we have four options: to recycle it, donate it, return it to the manufacturer or throw it in the trash. Most commonly, we throw it away.\" , \"start_char\": 1088.000000 , \"title\": \"Effects of E-Waste On Our Environment | AG Electronics </cover>Recycling\" , \"url\": \"https://allgreenrecycling.com/effects-of-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"26/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_828461508#2_1571848270","relevance":2,"reason":"The page is mainly about environmental effects of e-waste rather than being dedicated to disposal steps. It does directly mention that retired computers can be recycled, donated, returned to the manufacturer, or thrown in the trash, and argues for proper recycling, but the actionable answer is limited and buried in an effects-focused document.","learned":"E-waste effect pages may mention disposal options such as recycling, donating, manufacturer return, and trash, but are less focused than how-to disposal guidance."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"electronics donation and recycling\" data erase)","reason":"Search specifically for EPA-style electronics donation and recycling guidance that should contain authoritative step-by-step advice, including data removal before donation or recycling."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"electronics donation and recycling\" data erase)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1347594534#7_2776304880","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3598","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1347594534#7_2776304880\" , \"end_char\": 4044.000000 , \"headings\": \"How To Protect Your Data Before You Get Rid of Your Computer How To Protect Your <cover>Data Before You Get Rid of Your Computer Back Up Your Information Sign Out of Accounts, Disconnect Devices, and Erase Your Hard Drive Safely Dispose of Your Computer \" , \"segment\": \"Most computers contain hazardous materials like heavy metals that can contaminate the earth and don’t belong in a landfill. So what are your options? You can recycle or donate your computer. Computer manufacturers, electronics stores, and other organizations have computer recycling or donation programs. Check out the Environmental Protection Agency's Electronics Donation and Recycling </cover>page to learn about recycling or donating your computer. You saved your personal information. Wiped your hard drive clean. And safely disposed of the old computer. Now learn how to protect your new computer from hackers with these computer security tips. Tagged with:\" , \"start_char\": 3389.000000 , \"title\": \"How To "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"27/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1347594534#7_2776304880","relevance":3,"reason":"The page is dedicated to protecting data before getting rid of a computer and directly gives disposal steps: back up information, sign out of accounts, disconnect devices, erase the hard drive, then safely dispose of the computer through recycling or donation programs such as manufacturers, electronics stores, or EPA-linked options.","learned":"Before disposing of a computer, individuals should back up personal information, sign out of accounts, disconnect paired devices, erase the hard drive, and then recycle or donate through reputable programs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3598","reason":"This targeted query found a directly relevant data-protection and disposal page; continue for more authoritative electronics donation/recycling guidance."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3598"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1347594534#6_2776303334","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3598","rank":2,"snippet":"erase your computer’s hard drive. Look for a program or function on your computer that will let you <cover>erase all your files from the hard drive and reset it to factory settings. If it doesn’t have one, look for expert reviews online to see what programs are out there and which ones are compatible with the type of computer and hard drive you have. Safely Dispose of Your Computer You’ve saved your personal information and wiped your hard drive clean. Now you’re ready to get rid of that computer. Most computers contain hazardous materials like heavy metals that can contaminate the earth and don’t belong in a landfill. So what are your options? You can recycle or donate your computer. Computer manufacturers, electronics stores, and other organizations have computer recycling or donation programs. Check out the Environmental Protection Agency's Electronics Donation and Recycling page to learn about recycling or donating your computer.\" , \"start_char\": 2886.000000 , \"title\": \"How To Protect Your Data </cover>Before You Get Rid of Your Computer | FTC Consumer Information\" , \"url\": \"https://www.consumer."}}
